        },
        "semanticLevel": "detail",
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='width:100%;height:100%;overflow:auto;box-sizing:border-box;container-type:inline-size;background:#ffffff;font-family:Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#0f172a'><div style='box-sizing:border-box;max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;padding:0 clamp(20px,5cqw,40px) 64px'><div style='padding-top:32px'><header data-role='masthead'><div style='padding:10px 0;border-top:3px solid #0f172a;border-bottom:1px solid #0f172a;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>PRIMITIVE · import, export</div><h1 style='margin:40px 0 0;font:500 clamp(40px,9.5cqw,72px)/.98 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;letter-spacing:-.025em;text-wrap:balance;color:#0f172a'>Import and Export</h1><p style='margin:18px 0 0;max-width:36ch;font:italic 400 clamp(20px,3.6cqw,26px)/1.35 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#475569'>How one graph draws on another, and what it offers in return.</p></header></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><div style='display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:flex-start;gap:32px 40px'><div style='flex:999 1 380px;min-width:0'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Meaning</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>What is this thing?</strong></p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>An import is where a graph uses something from another graph: a class, a set of classes, or a whole graph, with the permissions it has been granted over it. An export is where a graph offers something to whoever imports it. The two are the ends of a Bridge, and a Bridge is the connection between them.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Cross-graph coupling is never implicit. A custom node type used anywhere in a graph must trace back to an import that grants it. If it can&#39;t, that&#39;s not a documentation gap: the graph lacks the authority it&#39;s exercising.</p></section></div><div style='flex:1 1 220px;min-width:0'><section data-role='settings' style='padding-top:10px;border-top:3px solid #0f172a'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#0f172a'>At a glance</h2><dl style='margin:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:max-content 1fr;column-gap:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #cbd5e1'><dt style='padding:9px 0 9px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Node types</dt><dd style='margin:0;padding:9px 0;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;text-align:right;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>import, export</dd><dt style='padding:9px 0 9px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Shown when browsing</dt><dd style='margin:0;padding:9px 0;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;text-align:right;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>No, only when editing</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Points at</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>a class, a set of classes, or a whole graph</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>On the border</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>imports left, exports right</dd><dt style='grid-column:1/-1;padding:9px 0 0px;border-top:1px solid #cbd5e1;font:400 13px/20px system-ui;color:#475569'>Joined by</dt><dd style='grid-column:1/-1;margin:0;padding:2px 0 9px;font:600 14px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>a Bridge, drawn from an export to the import that uses it</dd></dl></section></div></div></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Invariants</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>What makes this thing an import or an export, specifically?</strong></p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>A node is an <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>import</code> or an <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>export</code>, never both. Which end it is comes from its type, not a stored role. An import and an export are separate nodes even when they name the same <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>It names a <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind</code>: <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>node-class</code> (one custom node type), <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>library</code> (a bundle; an export of this kind with a class list is read by the create menu, and by nothing else — see Behavior), or <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>graph</code> (a whole graph, usually a template).</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>It names a <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code> — the concrete address of what it reaches.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>For <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: node-class</code>, the granted type key must actually be set. An import with a <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code> but no key grants nothing the validator can see, even though the UI may show it as configured.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Its <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>grants</code> say what&#39;s permitted over the target (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>read</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>create</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>update</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>execute</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>mutate</code>) — not every grant is meaningful for every <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind</code>.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><strong>The validator checks these</strong> (a warning when a graph loads, an error when it is edited or saved): an import or export with no <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code> (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>BRIDGE_REF_MISSING</code>); an import of a class with no <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>key</code> (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>BRIDGE_CLASS_KEY_MISSING</code>); and a node of a class, named by its <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.classRef</code>, in a graph that imports neither that class nor a graph or kit that hands classes on (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>CLASS_WITHOUT_IMPORT</code>). A class that has loaded for another graph does not count: a graph can use only the classes of its own imports.</li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Contract</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>What data belongs to it?</strong></p><pre style='margin:0 0 16px;padding:14px 16px;overflow-x:auto;white-space:pre;background:#f1f5f9;border-left:3px solid #6d28d9;font:400 13px/1.55 ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;color:#0f172a'>bridge   { ref, resourceKind, scope, grants[], key?, exposure?, entryPort?, subscriptions? }</pre><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>One record, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.bridge</code>, and each fact once. <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>key</code> is the class key an import of a class grants; <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>exposure</code> is what an export offers; <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>entryPort</code> is the port an import enters by. Nothing else is stored: whether it is an import or an export is its type; the endpoint is the <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code> and the entry port; there is no second record, and no copy of its settings at the top of <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data</code>. A loaded import carries those copies for the code that still reads them, and a saved one drops them. <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/bridgeRecord.js</code> does both, and <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>readBridgeSettings()</code> in <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>bridgeSettings.js</code> is the one place that reads a record for the editor.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Required at minimum: the node type (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>import</code> or <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>export</code>), <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code>. For <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: node-class</code>, also <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>key</code>.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>A node made from an imported class stores only its type (the class key) and <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>data.classRef</code>, the address of the class. It carries no copy of the class and no record of the import it came through; the app finds both when the graph loads. Grants and subscriptions are different contracts.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Ports and Relationships</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>An import or an export states cross-graph authority and participation. Its structural root socket and authored class/resource contract are distinct from the root&#39;s presentation Surface chains. Imports own cross-graph event subscriptions; exports expose emitter contracts. The Bridge drawn in the workspace is derived from an import and the export it uses, not a new authority record.</p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Port/handle projections may be materialized by the runtime. Do not infer that every import is unconnected or that a Bridge line itself persists a grant.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Behavior</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>What does it participate in?</strong></p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An <strong>import</strong> with <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: node-class</code> grants this graph permission to create instances of that type. <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>getBridgeNodeClassKey()</code> in the validator reads it directly off the graph&#39;s own node list — no fetch, no recursion.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An import with <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: graph</code>, when dragged onto a canvas, resolves the target&#39;s surfaces into one bound node (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>deriveGraphBackedNodeArtifact()</code> in <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>graphNodeContract.js</code>) — the target&#39;s ports and faces become that node&#39;s ports and faces.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An import with <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: graph</code> that points at a template reaches every class the template hands on. The template has one export of its own (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: graph</code>, <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code> the template itself) whose <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>exposure.nodeClasses</code> lists the class keys it offers. The classes are used in place: nothing is copied into the importing graph, which holds the one import and no more. Classes pass up the chain, so a template may list classes it reaches through its own import of another template.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An <strong>export</strong> says what this graph offers to a graph that imports it.<ul style='margin:6px 0 0;padding-left:22px;'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An export of the graph itself lists the classes it hands on. This is the current form.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An export that names a single class offers that class. This is the older form, and it still works.</li></ul></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>When both graphs are open in the same workspace, the import is drawn as one edge to the export it plugs into: the import on the left of its graph, the export on the right of the template. That edge is the Bridge; it is drawn from the two ends and is not stored.</li></ul><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An import that grants <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>execute</code> also lets a Method in this graph run a Method the template hands on. The template lists those on the same export (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>exposure.methods</code>); the Method node here holds no source and names this import.</li></ul><h3 style='margin:22px 0 8px;font:700 15px/20px system-ui;color:#0f172a'>Implemented event behavior</h3><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Imports own subscriptions built by bridgeEventSubscriptions; emitter contracts are read by eventBridge. Event Handler resolution follows the action-method output to ordered Methods. These responsibilities are separate from the geometry of the Bridge drawn in the workspace.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Known gaps</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Resource-kind support depends on the actual consumer. The library-kind reservation above remains a limitation, not a promised expansion path. Runtime delivery and browser connection behavior require their own smoke evidence.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Representations</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>How is it represented?</strong></p><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>An import or an export typically renders as a small authority card naming its end, resourceKind and target ref — never the target&#39;s own content. It points at what it grants or offers; it doesn&#39;t inline it. Collapsed, it shows just enough to answer &quot;what does this graph have authority over, and in which direction,&quot; without requiring a fetch of the target to render at all.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Explicitly Not Part of This Primitive</h2><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>An import doesn&#39;t fetch or copy what it names. Resolving a ref into actual nodes is the resolver&#39;s job (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>GraphCrud.js</code>&#39;s <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>bridgeReference()</code>), triggered by an action on the import, not performed by the node as data.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>The Bridge drawn between an import and an export isn&#39;t stored, and it isn&#39;t a grant. It exists only while both graphs are open together (see Behavior).</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Neither end is a Portal. A Portal opens onto a graph; an import or an export grants authority over one. The two can point at the same <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>ref</code> for entirely different reasons, and neither implies the other.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'>Older node-type dependency lists grant no authority. Imports and class bindings supply the current authority path.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: library</code> is a real, declared option in <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>BRIDGE_KINDS</code>, and the create menu reads one: an export of this kind hands on the classes its <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>exposure</code> lists. The validator&#39;s authority check does not read it, exactly as it would not read an unkeyed <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>node-class</code> import. Treat it as partly supported; an export of the graph itself (<code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: graph</code>) with a class list is the form to author.</li></ul></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Examples</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><strong>Examples live outside the primitive, referenced, not embedded.</strong></p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>templates/page-template/root.node</code> — four imports for its own classes (Masthead, Route, Settings, Section) and one export for the template, listing all four. A graph that uses it holds a single <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>resourceKind: graph</code> import of the template and can create all four.</li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>events/apollo-11.node</code>&#39;s <code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>event-class-bridge-event</code> node — a plain, single-class import with every field populated, worth reading as the minimal correct shape.</li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>The Page Template is linked from this page.</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:48px'><section data-role='section'><h2 style='margin:0 0 14px;font:800 12px/16px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#6d28d9'>Where this lives</h2><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Source (twilite repository; the files still carry the old name):</p><ul style='margin:0 0 14px;padding-left:22px;max-width:60ch;font:400 18px/1.55 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/importExport.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/bridgeSettings.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/eventBridge.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>components/GraphEditor/utils/graphNodeContract.js</code></li><li style='margin:0 0 6px'><code style='padding:1px 4px;border-radius:3px;background:#f1f5f9;font:400 .84em ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,Consolas,monospace;overflow-wrap:anywhere'>tests/bridgeSettings.test.js</code></li></ul><p style='margin:0 0 14px;max-width:60ch;font:400 19px/1.6 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;color:#1e293b'>Focused tests:</p></section></div><div style='padding-top:40px'><div style='display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:stretch;gap:28px 32px'><div style='flex:1 1 190px;min-width:0'><section data-role='route' data-prominence='primary' style='height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;display:flex;flex-direction:column;padding-top:12px;border-top:3px solid #6d28d9'><h2 style='margin:0 0 8px;font:500 26px/1.1 Newsreader,Iowan Old Style,Palatino Linotype,Book Antiqua,Georgia,serif;letter-spacing:-.01em;color:#0f172a'>Page Template</h2><p style='margin:0 0 10px;font:400 14px/1.55 system-ui;color:#475569'>A template with four class imports and one export.</p><a data-role='route-link' href='tlz://twilite.zone/library/templates/page-template/root.node' style='margin-top:auto;font:700 13px/20px system-ui;text-decoration:none;color:#6d28d9'>Open the template →</a></section></div></div></div></div></div>"
