    {
      "id": "bridge-meaning",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Meaning",
      "position": {
        "x": 430,
        "y": -1636
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Meaning",
        "body": "**What is this thing?**\n\nAn import is where a graph uses something from another graph: a class, a set of classes, or a whole graph, with the permissions it has been granted over it. An export is where a graph offers something to whoever imports it. The two are the ends of a Bridge, and a Bridge is the connection between them.\n\nCross-graph coupling is never implicit. A custom node type used anywhere in a graph must trace back to an import that grants it. If it can't, that's not a documentation gap: the graph lacks the authority it's exercising.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
          "state": "auto-placed",
          "source": "bridge",
          "updatedAt": 1791592047773,
          "reason": "bridge-reference"
