    {
      "id": "bridge-behavior",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Behavior",
      "position": {
        "x": 3230,
        "y": -1636
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Behavior",
        "body": "**What does it participate in?**\n\n- An **import** with `resourceKind: node-class` grants this graph permission to create instances of that type. `getBridgeNodeClassKey()` in the validator reads it directly off the graph's own node list — no fetch, no recursion.\n- An import with `resourceKind: graph`, when dragged onto a canvas, resolves the target's surfaces into one bound node (`deriveGraphBackedNodeArtifact()` in `graphNodeContract.js`) — the target's ports and faces become that node's ports and faces.\n- An import with `resourceKind: graph` that points at a template reaches every class the template hands on. The template has one export of its own (`resourceKind: graph`, `ref` the template itself) whose `exposure.nodeClasses` lists the class keys it offers. The classes are used in place: nothing is copied into the importing graph, which holds the one import and no more. Classes pass up the chain, so a template may list classes it reaches through its own import of another template.\n- An **export** says what this graph offers to a graph that imports it.\n  - An export of the graph itself lists the classes it hands on. This is the current form.\n  - An export that names a single class offers that class. This is the older form, and it still works.\n- When both graphs are open in the same workspace, the import is drawn as one edge to the export it plugs into: the import on the left of its graph, the export on the right of the template. That edge is the Bridge; it is drawn from the two ends and is not stored.\n\n- An import that grants `execute` also lets a Method in this graph run a Method the template hands on. The template lists those on the same export (`exposure.methods`); the Method node here holds no source and names this import.\n\n## Implemented event behavior\n\nImports own subscriptions built by bridgeEventSubscriptions; emitter contracts are read by eventBridge. Event Handler resolution follows the action-method output to ordered Methods. These responsibilities are separate from the geometry of the Bridge drawn in the workspace.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "_placement": {
          "version": 1,
